    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d310">

                <pb facs="0392" n="272" sameAs="frus:frus1931v02/pg272"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">690d.11241/12</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Chargé in France</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Armour</hi>)</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-09-24">September 24, 1929</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 4258</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: The Department has received your despatches
                    No. 9793 of August 27, 1929<anchor n="59" xml:id="d310-f1"/> and No. 9819 of
                    September 6, 1929, with regard to the customs immunity of American philanthropic
                    and educational institutions in Syria and the Lebanon.</p>
                <p>In view of the fact that the Foreign Office Note of August 22, 1929,<anchor
                        n="60" xml:id="d310-f2"/> enclosed with the despatch first mentioned above,
                    fails to give consideration to the proposal submitted to the French High
                    Commission in Beirut by Mr. Knabenshue on August 30, 1928, the Department <pb
                        facs="0393" n="273" xml:id="pg273"/> would be glad to have you take
                    opportunity again to express to the Foreign Office the hope that it may be found
                    possible to give further study to Mr. Knabenshue’s proposal.</p>
                <p>I am [etc.]</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Nelson
                            Trusler Johnson</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
